    "What is your relationship with mathematics?": "I’ve always found math intriguing, especially when it connects to real-world problems. While it’s not my favorite subject compared to chemistry or philosophy, I enjoy how patterns and logic can help us understand things. I see it as a tool, not a barrier, and appreciate how it helps build problem-solving skills.",
    "Do you ever get anxious when thinking about mathematics?": "Not usually, but I do get a bit tense when I’m stuck on a complex problem. I think it’s just part of the learning process. I try to break things down and stay patient. My parents, especially my dad with his PhD, have always encouraged a calm approach to challenges, which helps me stay grounded.",
    "Did you ever use AI to support your math learning in the last year? If yes, how was your experience?": "Yes, I used an AI tutor for algebra. It was really helpful, especially for explaining step-by-step solutions. It gave me immediate feedback and allowed me to retry problems until I understood. I found it a little overwhelming at first, but it made learning more interactive and less intimidating.",
    "How would you explain, step by step, how to solve a second order algebraic equation?": "Start by writing the equation in standard form: ax² + bx + c = 0. Then use the quadratic formula: x = (-b ± √(b² - 4ac)) / 2a. Plug in the values for a, b, and c. Calculate the discriminant first. If it’s positive, you get two real solutions. If zero, one solution. If negative, complex solutions. Always check your answers by plugging them back into the original equation.",
    "How would you explain, step by step, how to find the stationary points of an equation y=f(x)?": "First, take the derivative of the function, f'(x). Then set f'(x) equal to zero and solve for x. These x-values are potential stationary points. Plug them back into the original function to find the corresponding y-values. Finally, use the second derivative test: if f''(x) > 0, it's a local minimum; if f''(x) < 0, a local maximum. If f''(x) = 0, it's an inflection point.",
